<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wærferth of Worcester</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 915</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Werferth</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wærferth was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a> from about 872 to 915, one of four Mercian scholars whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> brought to his court to revive learning. At Alfred's request he translated Pope <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Gregory the Great</a>'s Dialogues into Old English. He also worked with Æthelred and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelflaed">Æthelflæd</a>, the rulers of Mercia, in fortifying Worcester as a burh.</p></div>
